From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: do not discard a patch that already answered 0x07

With check_crc set, the ROM now answers PATCH_FINISH_REQ before it jumps:

  diag fwdl done: tail=4 head=4 didx=4
  diag mcu resp cmd=0x07 seq=7 len=0     <- CRC accepted, jumping
  Failed to release patch semaphore, sem=-110

so the image did reach MCU address 0x00900000 intact and the CRC in its
header matches.  What fails next is the semaphore release, and that is
expected rather than broken: PATCH_SEM_CONTROL is a ROM command, and once the
ROM has jumped into the patch the MCU belongs to the patch, which has no
reason to keep answering it.  The driver currently treats that timeout as
fatal and returns -EAGAIN, throwing away a patch that is already running, so
the RAM code download never even starts.

Warn and keep going.  If the patch really is dead the scatter that follows
fails immediately - its didx stays at 0 - which is a much clearer signal than
a semaphore timeout on a command the patch was never going to serve.

This supersedes 044: that one only removed the goto that retried the failing
write forever, this one removes the failure itself.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
@@ -377,9 +377,15 @@
 	case PATCH_REL_SEM_SUCCESS:
 		break;
 	default:
-		ret = -EAGAIN;
-		dev_err(dev->dev, "Failed to release patch semaphore\n");
-		goto out;
+		/*
+		 * The patch owns the MCU once the ROM has jumped into it, and it
+		 * stops answering the ROM's semaphore command: 0x07 comes back as
+		 * an ACK and the very next command times out.  Treating that as
+		 * fatal throws away a patch that is already running, so only warn
+		 * here and let the RAM code download decide whether it is alive.
+		 */
+		dev_warn(dev->dev,
+			 "patch sem release failed (sem=%d), continuing\n", sem);
 	}
 	if (mcu_entry->applied_method == BIN_METHOD)
 		release_firmware(fw);
